COOK_NOTES.update({
'37.1':"""Both **hęriað** forms are plural commands. **Dryhten** and **hine** are the persons praised, in the accusative. **Of heofonum** gives the origin and **on hēanessum** the location, both with dative plurals. Adding a second-person subject or an article would miss the whole exercise's omission rule; treating praise as an assertion would change its force.""",
'37.2':"""The angels and powers are the groups addressed, not extra objects of praise. **Ealle ęnglas** is masculine plural; **eall mægnu** is neuter plural, with the explicitly supplied sense “powers.” **His** belongs to both groups. Neither the English collective sense nor the familiar strength gloss licenses a singular reading here.""",
'37.3':"""Sun and moon are singular entities in the address; **steorran** is plural, while **lēoht** is the supplied mass noun “light.” **Ealle** modifies the stars. Keep both commands and all four named entities. English ye does not override the instruction to omit the second-person pronoun.""",
'37.4':"""**Heofona** is genitive plural dependent on the addressed **heofonas**. **Wæteru** supplies the plural antecedent of **ðe**, the subject of **sindon** inside the relative clause. The selected **ofer heofonas** retains the local accusative target. A genitive near the relative does not determine its internal subject role.""",
'37.5':"""**Hęrien hīe** is a third-plural wish, “may they praise,” rather than a command addressed to them or a permission construction. **Naman** is the accusative object and **Dryhtnes** its genitive dependent. Keep the specified **gecwæð** and both passive predicates. **Worhte/gesceapene** are selected plural predicate participles after **wǣron**; worhte here is not a second finite past verb. Uninflected predicate participles are also attested, but the model illustrates the assigned plural forms.""",
'37.6':"""The two **sętte** predicates appoint the entities and establish a decree. The simple preterite may render English hath appointed; the second clause expressly omits hath. The final understood subject is **bebod**, the decree, not God or plural hīe. **Ne gewīt** has future reference here. The entire compressed ending and **ā ā ā** remain supplied; restoring it would miss the explicit omission.""",
'37.7':"""**Dracan** and **ealla nēowolnessa** are plural addressees; **Dryhten** is the object praised. **Of eorðan** gives the origin. The selected feminine plural quantifier is ealla. A documented -e variant need not be ungrammatical, but differs from that selected table form. Do not turn the dragons and deeps into the things being praised.""",
'37.8':"""This catalogue continues the preceding address. **Ȳsta** is genitive plural, “of storms”; **ðe** represents the spirits as the subject of plural **dōð**. **His word** is their accusative object in the taught fulfilment sense of dōn. The source fragment needs neither an invented new main verb nor an added “things” noun for the listed substances.""",
'37.9':"""All four groups remain plural. **Wæstmbǣran** is the selected weak adjective in direct address, agreeing with **trēowu**; the given dictionary form wæstmbǣre does not itself supply that ending. **Ealle** follows its own strong pattern with hills and cedars. Strong **wæstmbǣru** is a real neuter plural form, but does not instantiate the selected weak-address construction. Keep short **æ** in wæstm.""",
'37.10':"""**Dēor** is endingless neuter plural here; **eall nīetenu** also has neuter plural agreement. **Nædran** names the serpents. The birds are plural **fuglas**, with supplied weak address adjective **gefiðredan**. English cattle and fowl do not independently determine OE gender or allow singular agreement. The parenthetical wild does not require adding a separate adjective to dēor.""",
'37.11':"""Kings, peoples, princes and judges form four addressed plural groups. **Eorðan** depends on kings and judges where it occurs. **Eall folc** is neuter plural despite the endingless noun; **ealle dēman** is masculine plural. Changing the outer case because a noun has a genitive dependent confuses the two relations.""",
'37.12':"""**Geongan** and **ealdan** are weak plural adjectives used for the addressed young and old people. Do not restore the expressly omitted men. **Fǣmnan** is the plural noun “maidens,” while **mid bearnum** is dative plural accompaniment. Calling the adjectives substantival explains their use without creating separate new nouns.""",
'37.13':"""The opening is again a third-plural wish. **His ānes** means “of him alone”; it does not mean merely “his only name.” **Ūpāhafen** is a passive participle after **is**, distinct from the finite present in 37.14. Glory is above both earth and heaven in the selected **ofer** phrase. Preserve all three predications and the causal connection.""",
'37.14':"""Retain literal **horn**. Raising it in this praise context can be understood as exalting the people's strength or power; that interpretation explains the image without establishing a replacement dictionary gloss. **Lof** is appositional praise, followed by the chain of genitives. **Hālgena** is from the explicitly cited weak noun **hālga**, not the adjective hālig. In **folces him nēalǣcendes**, the participle agrees with genitive singular folces, while him is the dative person approached. The whole dense phrase is supplied; removing the image or making him genitive loses a distinct relation.""",
'38.1':"""**Micel elpenbǣnen setl** is the neuter accusative throne, resumed by **hit**. **Oferworhte** meets the explicit oferwyrcean target; another grammatical verb for covering would not meet that lexical instruction. **Mid ðǣm bętstan golde** gives the material, with the selected weak superlative after the article. Keep both making and overlaying.""",
'38.2':"""The subject is feminine singular **sumu cwēn**; the existential dummy there is omitted. **On ðǣm dagum** and **be sūðan** are the supplied temporal and southern-location phrases. **Hāten Saba** identifies her name; it does not mean that she was ordered to act. The foreign name is local reference help, not an inferred productive naming paradigm.""",
'38.3':"""Solomon possesses the fame; the queen comes from the southern boundaries to Solomon within Jerusalem. **Olfendas** is the plural camel subject of **bǣron**. Preserve herbs, gems and the quantity of gold as three coordinated loads; **goldes** is genitive after **unrīm**. The local **sūðerne wyrta** uses an evidenced feminine plural -e variant, not an error merely because another selected table uses -a. The supplied Salomones and foreign place-name remain available.""",
'38.4':"""**Wið Salomon** is the supplied speech-partner frame, not hostility toward Solomon. **Him** is the dative person told; the content is whatever the queen thought. Keep **ðōhte** last in that subordinate clause, as directed, and retain the thought in her heart. A supported dative conversation-partner variant can be valid; it does not change the recipient/content distinction.""",
'38.5':"""The person taught is accusative **hīe**; the person told is dative **hiere**. **Andgiet** is the meaning communicated, with dependent **ealra ðāra worda**. Inside the relative, the queen is subject and **hine** the person she asked; **ðe** represents the things asked about. Its role is not determined simply by the genitive worda outside the clause.""",
'38.6':"""The queen sees three things: Solomon's wisdom, the temple, and the king's services. **Mǣre** retains the named adjective. The relative's **geworhte** is simple preterite under om. had; earlier building is inferred from the context, without adding a forbidden auxiliary. **Manigfealda ðēnunga** is the selected feminine accusative plural. **Tempel** remains the full nominative/accusative stem despite syncope in other cells.""",
'38.7':"""The supplied **tō ðan … ðæt** relates degree of astonishment to its result. **Ofwundrod** describes the astonished subject; **nānne gāst … næfde** is one negative predication, with accusative gāst. **Furðor** preserves the “more” reference. “She was left breathless” may explain the effect, but replacing the whole literal structure with a report of her death is unsupported.""",
'38.8':"""Keep both the truth of the report and the queen's former refusal to believe before seeing personally. **Self** remains: om. my does not omit it. **Gesāwe** is first-singular past subjunctive in the prospective before-clause viewed from that earlier refusal, not a claim that she never saw it. Only the final saw-clause is explicitly marked verb last; **hīerde** is also clause-final in this model, but that placement is not separately mandated. The supplied topic phrases do not make thee the object of hearing.""",
'38.9':"""**Māra** supplies the comparative, and the model treats wisdom with its work as a combined singular subject. A defensible plural matrix formulation can be grammatical if its agreement is coherent. The explicitly given **wǣre** belongs to singular **se hlīsa**, the report, not to the wisdom/work subject. Its exact spelling is visible reference help; this one supplied comparison does not require subjunctive after every ðonne.""",
'38.10':"""Both **ðegnas** and **ðēowan** are plural subjects of **sindon**, with plural **ēadige**. They also supply the relative subject of **standað** and **hīerað**. **Ætforan ðē** locates them before the addressee; **ðīnne wīsdōm** is the masculine accusative object heard. Preserve always and both groups rather than reducing the statement to one servant.""",
'39.1':"""**Blētsod sīe** expresses the blessing wish. God is the relative subject who chose and placed the addressee; **ðē** is accusative in those actions. The final **ðæt** introduces purpose, and **sętte** is present subjunctive second singular with **ðū**, “that you establish.” It is not a new past assertion about God. Keep **gesętte**, the accusative authority phrase after ofer, and the omission of mightest.""",
'39.2':"""The king is dative recipient. The whole supplied amount is **hund and twēntig punda goldes**, with genitive pounds after the high number and genitive gold as material. **Męnigeo** has the dependent genitive spice and stone phrases. Retain all three gifts and omit the two articles as directed. This local 120 expression does not test a general high-numeral system.""",
'39.3':"""The queen is dative recipient of Solomon's gift; **eall ðæt** is its accusative object. Within the relative, **giernan** has the taught desired-thing relation and **æt him** names the person asked. The invariant ðe allows the inner relation without forcing the outer object into genitive. The return keeps both her own homeland and her accompanying retainers; changing the possessor to Solomon changes the event.""",
'39.4':"""**Ofer eallum eorðlicum cyningum** is the explicitly required dative rank comparison. The desire is expressed by the bracketed finite structure: **ðæt hīe … gesāwen … hīerden**, past subjunctive plurals after **gewilnodon**. An infinitive could express a similar meaning in an appropriate frame but misses this finite-clause target. **Ealla ðēoda** is the selected feminine plural subject.""",
'39.5':"""Keep all six materials and the dative foundation after the specified ofer. **Swā hwā swā** is the indefinite builder; **ānes gehwilces** is the exact supplied genitive “of each one,” dependent on **weorc**. **Bið gesweotolod** expresses the future manifestation of that work. In particular, the prompt says trees: substituting a different material sense is unnecessary. Replacing the given genitive phrase with a synonym can miss a reference target.""",
'39.6':"""Feminine **sēo getimbrung** is the subject of both present indicatives; this is an open condition. **Wiðstęnt** governs dative **ðǣm fȳre**. The workman receives dative **edlēane** in the selected onfōn frame, from **æt Gode**; **his weorces** gives the causal genitive “for his work.” A documented accusative receiving frame is not universally wrong, but differs from the selected dative construction here.""",
'39.7':"""Retain **mæg**: eye, ear and heart cannot see, hear or conceive the things prepared. The coordinated infinitives share that modal; **āsmēan** is a visible citation reference in a supplied frame. **Gearcað** is present, “prepares,” not a past preparation. The people who love God are dative beneficiaries without an added for. The repeated negatives express one negative scope, not cancellation.""",
'40.1':"""The two **wǣre** clauses report what the angel said; **hēo** is the filled person, while **God** is the subject of the second reported clause. Keep the postposed **hiere mid** as required. **Godes giefe** is the supplied grace/gift expression, not an independently inferred extension of an earlier gift gloss. The final **hēo wæs blētsod** is the separate assertion about Mary among women.""",
'40.2':"""**Mǣrsað** is present, magnifying in praise; **blissode** is preterite, rejoiced. The English helpers are omitted as directed. The whole **blissode on Gode mīnum Hǣlende** frame supplies rejoicing in God, with the dative apposition “my Saviour.” It neither describes physical enlargement of the Lord nor follows automatically from a previously known but different rejoicing preposition.""",
'40.3':"""**Geseah** has the taught regard sense, with the handmaid's humility as object. In the second clause **ealla cnēorissa** is feminine nominative plural and controls **sęcgað**. **Mē** is the person called, accusative, and **ēadige** is the agreeing feminine accusative description of Mary. The future reference does not require a future auxiliary. A saying/quotation interpretation that loses the object predicate misses “call me blessed.”""",
'40.4':"""**Se ðe mihtig is** names the mighty one; **mē** is the dative beneficiary of **dyde**, without the omitted to. **Miclu ðing** is neuter accusative plural, “great things.” Do not substitute a masculine plural adjective simply because English things is plural. **Hālig** is the predicate of singular **his nama**; both predications remain.""",
'40.5':"""**Ondrǣdendum** is a dative plural human substantive, the beneficiaries of mercy, while its internal object **hine** is accusative. Do not restore the expressly omitted on those. The complete **of cnēorisse on cnēorisse** phrase supplies the temporal range from generation to generation; its local uses are not inferred from spatial motion rules. The two case relations in the fear phrase match F05.""",
'40.6':"""**Ðā mihtgan** and **ðā ēaðmōdan** are weak accusative plural substantives, the people displaced and raised. The two strong preterites **āwearp** and **ūpāhōf** retain the reversal. **Of setle** is the source, with no possessive their under the omission control. Abstract “power” and “humility” would lose the affected people.""",
'40.7':"""**Ðā hyngrendan** is the hungry people as weak accusative plural. **Mid gōdum** uses the previously taught lexical noun “good things,” dative plural, without restoring the omitted things. **Ðā rīcan** is the object of **forlēt** in the let-go/dismiss sense; **ǣmtige** describes their condition. Do not turn hungry into an impersonal finite clause or make empty agree with singular hē.""",
'40.8':"""**Onfēng his cnihte Israel** uses the selected dative person-acceptance frame, with cniht in the servant sense and the supplied name Israel. **Gemunde** preserves the distinct remembering action and its object mercy. The attested care/protection sense explains the reception, but “helped” need not replace the prompt's accepted. The foreign name does not license an invented general declension.""",
'40.9':"""The comparison **swā** introduces what he spoke. **Tō** governs the fathers, Abraham and his seed; **his** refers to Abraham. The newly taught collective seed sense is offspring or descendants, not agricultural produce. Retaining “seed” preserves the image, while descendants is an acceptable explanatory translation. Keep the full perpetuity expression and do not turn the genitive possessor into the speaker.""",
'40.10':"""**Hiere mid** gives the woman with whom Mary stayed; **swilce** supplies approximation here. **Ðrīe mōnaðas** is accusative duration, not a location or a dative after mid. The destination **tō hiere āgnum hūse** is Mary's own house. The two actions are dwelling and returning; omitting own or assigning its possessor to the other woman changes the reference.""",
'41.1':"""Keep all three relations: God's everlasting existence, the everlasting begetting of his wisdom, and that wisdom's identity with his Son. The supplied **of him ācęnned** is this specific source relation, not a general newly taught passive-agent rule. **Swā mihtig swā** expresses equality with the Father. Nothing in the repeated ever requires inventing a chronological beginning for the begetting.""",
'41.2':"""**Gefylð** uses the fill sense, with accusative **ealne ymbhwyrft middangeardes**, the entire circuit of the world. **Hielt** is the selected present third singular of healdan in the explicitly taught sustain/maintain sense. **Eall ðing** is neuter accusative plural. Finishing a task and merely grasping objects would substitute different senses for the two predicates.""",
'41.3':"""**Se ðe** names whoever performs the Father's will; **hē** resumes that subject in the main clause. **Mīnes Fæder willan** is the accusative will with its genitive possessor. Keep all three predicate relations—brother, mother and sister. The figurative kinship does not require altering the grammar to claim literal biological identity.""",
'41.4':"""The first clause denies bringing anything into the world; the second denies ability to carry anything away. **Brōhton** is past plural; **magon … lǣdan** is present plural modal plus infinitive. **Nān … ne** preserves negative concord, and **ūs mid** means with us. Do not replace the carrying-things sense with an unrelated leading-people interpretation or omit hence.""",
'41.5':"""Keep all three command/result pairs. **Biddað, sēcað, cnuciað** address several people; **gē findað** asserts the resulting finding with future reference. **Ēow bið seald/geopenod** are the supplied passives with dative recipients. Om. it shall does not remove the OE finite passive auxiliary: it prevents a mechanical English calque. Restoring a dummy it or a preposition before ēow misses the stated targets.""",
'41.6':"""**Ūre Fæder** is direct address. The relative refers to **ðū**, so its verb is second-singular **eart**, despite English which. The name is the subject of the wish **sīe … gehālgod**. A third-person relative verb or a plain assertion that the name is holy would miss the specified person or the wish.""",
'41.7':"""Both clauses are wishes, with present subjunctive verbs first. **Tōcume** retains the explicitly required prefix; bare cuman may be grammatical in another formulation but does not meet that lexical target. **Geweorðe** means come to pass here. Keep will as the second subject and the supplied **swā-swā** comparison between earth and heavens.""",
'41.8':"""**Sęle** and **forgief** are singular commands, addressed to God; **ūs** is dative. The daily bread is accusative **ūrne dæghwāmlīcan hlāf**, using the indicated weak adjective choice. **Gyltas** are the debts, but **gyltendum** are the dative debtors forgiven in the comparison clause. **Wē … forgiefað** is first-plural assertion. Exchanging debts and debtors changes both participants and government.""",
'41.9':"""**Ne lǣd ðū** is a negative singular command; **ālīes** is its positive counterpart, deliver. **Ūs** is accusative in both actions. **On costnunge** gives the directed temptation phrase, while **of yfele** gives the source of deliverance. Do not turn these into assertions about what God is doing or reverse the two prepositional relations.""",
'41.10':"""**Sīe** is present subjunctive third singular in an independent wish or assent formula, with **hit** as subject: “May it be so.” A simple “It is so” would turn the wish into an assertion. No permission verb is needed.""",
'41.11':"""The father gives to dative **his bearne**. Inside the condition **hit** refers to that child and is the asker; **hine** is the father asked, accusative, and **hlāfes** the thing requested, genitive. **Bitt** agrees with the singular child. Giving and asking therefore have different case frames; English of does not require an added OE preposition.""",
'41.12':"""**Āwrāt** retains the prefixed write lexeme and its strong past stem. **Ðā ealdan ǣ** is the law written; **mid his fingre** is the instrument. The tablets are the dative plural surface **on ðǣm stānenum tabelum**, with stānen specifying stone material. Do not reinterpret tables as furniture or make God's finger the thing written.""",
'42.1':"""The supplied idiom makes **lufu**, love, the grammatical subject; **ðæs geongan mannes** is its genitive possessor. **On ðæt mǣden** is the accusative person toward whom the love falls. **Befēoll** retains the exercise's be-prefix and the checked long past vowel. A translation with the man as literal falling subject misses the idiom, even if modern English says “the man fell in love.”""",
'42.2':"""The speaker hears; the harpers harp and sing. **Hearpera** and **hearpiendra** are genitive plural around accusative **stefne**; **hiera hearpum** is dative after mid. The new song is accusative **nīwne sang**; **ðone sang** refers back to it. Preserve the throne, four beasts and negative learning clause. The whole **būton … ðūsendo** expression supplies exactly 144,000; neither its ordering nor its unfamiliar shapes are recall tests. Do not label that whole exception dative or derive a general būton case rule from it.""",
'42.3':"""The seen company, **micle męnigu**, is accusative feminine singular. The subsequent people stand, are clothed, and hold palm twigs; **ealle**, plural **gescrȳdde**, and **hiera** refer to the members by sense. Keep both origin phrases and all three descriptions. **Palmtwīg** is accusative plural despite its zero ending. This complete supplied collective construction does not establish a general freedom to mix singular and plural agreement.""",
'42.4':"""The singers are plural, but the embedded praise is **Hǣlu sīe ūrum Gode**, with God as dative recipient and singular sīe. **Mid hlūdre stefne** gives the loud manner of singing. In the relative, God is the singular subject of **sitt**, and the prescribed **ofer his ðrymsetle** is dative. Keep the praise formula rather than substituting an unrelated rescue event.""",
'42.5':"""The angels stand in the circuit of the throne and bow to dative **Gode**. **Āluton** is the plural strong preterite of the intransitive bow verb, not the transitive bend verb used in 42.8. **Ðus cweðende** accompanies their actions and introduces the following speech. It is a participle, not an additional finite verb with its own tense.""",
'42.6':"""Retain all six coordinated qualities: blessing, brightness, wisdom, thanksgiving, dignity and strength, together with the dative recipient and perpetuity formula. The supplied doxology has singular optative **sīe**. Its local formula does not establish a universal singular-agreement rule for every list of subjects. Omitting a near-synonym still loses a requested element.""",
'42.7':"""**Holu** and **nest** are different neuter accusative plural patterns; the second possession clause shares the first clause's have. **Næfð hwǣr … āhylde** supplies the negative indefinite-place frame: he has no place where he might lay his head. **Āhylde** is present subjunctive third singular, without a separate may. **Hwǣr** is dependent here, not a direct question asking where he is.""",
'42.8':"""The soldiers plait, place, and bend their knee; keep each action and the shared crown object where the English omits repetition. **Of ðornum** gives material; **on his hēafod** is the placement goal. The supplied **on his swīðran** names his right hand without restoring the omitted noun. **Hiera cnēo** retains the singular knee in the prompt. The final salute addresses one man as King of the Jews; it is the soldiers' speech, not the narrator's approval.""",
'42.9':"""The same plural subject performs three past actions: **spǣtton**, **nōmon**, **bēoton**. **On hine** is the target of spitting; **hrēod** is the reed taken; **his hēafod** is the direct object struck. Preserve that change of object. Bēatan means beat/strike here; replacing it with a killing sense would add an outcome the prompt does not state.""",
'43.1':"""**Ǣlc flǣsc is gærs** retains the each-flesh/grass image. **Ðæs flǣsces wuldor** is the glory of the flesh, compared by **swilce** with the blossom of the plants. The whole figurative comparison is supplied. “Human life is brief” can explain an implication but is not a complete translation of both clauses. See A05 for the distinction between their structures.""",
'43.2':"""**Nān mann ne hīerde** is one negation: no one heard. **His stefne** is the accusative thing heard, while **on strǣtum** is the dative place, without the expressly omitted article. The negative words do not cancel one another. A paraphrase that restores the article may convey the meaning but misses the omission target.""",
'43.3':"""Keep all three singular commands: cry, do not cease, lift up the voice. **Cleopa** uses the cry-aloud sense; **ðīne stefne** is accusative. **Swā-swā bieman** is the supplied elliptical trumpet comparison. The exact comparative cue remains visible. It compares the lifted voice to the trumpet image; it does not replace voice by an actual trumpet as the command's object.""",
'43.4':"""**Hlǣfdige** addresses the lady; **hwider** asks direction, whither. **Gǣst ðū** is second-singular present. A static where-question would change the requested destination sense, while a plural verb would change the addressee's number.""",
'43.5':"""**Dēst** is second-singular present, and **yfele** is the adverb ill/badly, describing the action. The sentence says that the addressee acts badly; it is not a predicate-adjective construction requiring an adjective to agree with thou.""",
'43.6':"""Both **dyde** and **tǣlde** are past singular, connected causally. **Ðā cwēn** is the selected endingless accusative with the specifically supplied princess sense. **Ðæs cyninges dohtor** identifies that princess in apposition. Rendering her merely as a queen would lose the contextual sense even though the headword can bear it elsewhere.""",
'43.7':"""**Leornodon** is preterite plural. **Nǣfre … ne** supplies never, and **wel** retains the quality of the learning. **Gerīmcræft** is arithmetic, not any unspecified learning. Neither the two negative expressions nor the later adverb cancels the negative scope.""",
'43.8':"""Both subjects are plural **gē**, so both finite verbs are **witon**. The first present has future reference with soon; the second, with **gīet ne**, states what is not yet known. The dependent **hwæt** clause is the content of that knowledge. No future auxiliary or independently recalled contracted nytan form is required.""",
'43.9':"""**Plega** is the selected singular command; **mid ðoðore** is the dative ball used in the play frame. The English prompt does not fix the number of listeners, so an accurately formed plural command is also defensible with that setting. The parenthetical with is an English construction cue, not an OE dictionary form or a demand to invent a different ball noun.""",
'43.10':"""The speaker's **ne ete** is first-singular present indicative under nān ðing. The others have third-plural **eten** and **sīen**, present subjunctives in the admitted concession. This mood does not say that they fail to eat or be merry. The supplied **ðēah ðe** is the conjunction although; its second element is not a relative pronoun meaning who.""",
'43.11':"""**Ic gewilnie** introduces the explicitly requested finite content clause, with first-singular subjunctive **leornie**. **Of ēow** names the plural people from whom the speaker learns. The knowledge is the antecedent of **ðe**, with **gē habbað** in the relative. An infinitive could express a desire to learn in another construction but misses the bracketed finite-clause target here.""",
'43.12':"""**Cēos** commands one addressee. **Hwilc** represents the choice; **hiera** is a partitive genitive, of them. **Wille** is present subjunctive second singular in the indefinite choice clause. It is not an English-style future auxiliary, and the genitive is not an adjective agreeing with the selected object.""",
'43.13':"""**Binnan fēawum tīdum** supplies the temporal limit. **Twēgen** agrees with masculine **męnn**; both **gelǣrede** and **æðelborene** are plural descriptions of those men. Gelǣrede is the already taught lexical adjective educated/trained. **Him tōgēanes** expresses motion toward him, not merely a static nearby position. Keep both education and birth.""",
'43.14':"""**Gierne** is first-singular present in the marriage-seeking sense. **Ðǣre æðelan fǣmnan** is the genitive maiden wooed, with weak adjective agreement. An accusative inferred from the modern English transitive verb would miss the taught giernan government. Familiar desire vocabulary alone would not establish this particular wooing sense.""",
'43.15':"""**Wē ealle** are the plural speakers. Within their report, **nān mann** is singular and **sīe** is present subjunctive third singular, as the source reference directs. **Gode** belongs to the supplied acceptable-to frame; **swā … swā hē** preserves the degree comparison with him. The subjunctive reports their judgment without proving that it is false; the negative stays inside the reported clause.""",
'43.16':"""**Hē** is the thinker, while indefinite **man** is the singular actor inside the reported clause and **hine** its accusative victim. **Slōge** is past subjunctive in the expected killing. An appropriate passive could preserve the broad meaning, but the explicit bracketed target requires this active impersonal formulation. The sentence does not assert that the killing occurred.""",
'43.17':"""**Wē ealle feallað** is first-plural present. **Tō ðīnum fōtum** has the dative plural feet but a singular addressee as their possessor. The contextual gesture is falling at that person's feet. A plural possessive would change the addressee, not merely agree more fully with plural fōtum.""",
'43.18':"""The speaker negates believing, **ic ne gelīefe**, without translating English do. In the content clause **ðū** controls second-singular subjunctive **sīe**; its spelling also serves other persons, but its subject fixes the analysis here. **Mīn wīf** is the predicate wife. Negated belief is not itself a proof that the reported proposition is false.""",
'43.19':"""**Hwǣr** asks location, with singular **is**. **Mīn wer** is the speaker's husband in the newly taught spouse sense. Whither would change a location question to one of direction; generic man alone would leave out the marital relation.""",
'43.20':"""**Lifde** is preterite first singular with ic. **Endleofan gēar** is the accusative duration, eleven years. **On Ægypta lande** is dative location with the supplied genitive plural ethnonym, “in the land of the Egyptians.” The duration is not another location, and the foreign people's name is not a test of an independently reconstructed ethnic paradigm.""",
})
